[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #1: Knight Placement on e5"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D02"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/3bpn2/3p1b2/3P1B2/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "5"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{[%evp 0,1,9,-2] The Jobava London did not catch on for most of chess history for one simple reason: you are not supposed to block the c-pawn in Queen's Pawn Openings. Why, in spite of being guilty of this cardinal sin, does the Jobava London "work?" The answer is wildly complex, but a big part of it lies in the tremendous potential of our three other minor pieces, in particular the king's knight. The king's knight is the centerpiece of White's kingside play, and it must be astutely deployed and exploited to its full potential if we are to justify the violation of one of the most basic chess principles. In the majority of Jobava lines, the knight best fulfills its potential on the e5 square. The position in front of you, which arises in the symmetrical variation (3...Bf5), affords a particularly instructive example of the importance of shepharding the knight to e5 early in the opening.} {[#]} 1. Ne5 {With this move, we accomplish a host of useful and important things: 1) The knight ensures the longevity of our dark-squared bishop, which itself can serve as a crucial attacker and can turn up the heat by sliding up to g5. 2) The knight interrupts the easy and natural flow of Black's development. Black can no longer develop his own knight without a second thought, and the pressure on the f7-pawn will cause Black headaches in the long run. 3) Capturing the knight with the bishop grants us the bishop pair, as well as full control over the dark squares.  I have deliberately left out one more thing, which constitutes the most important reasons for sticking the knight on e5 - an idea without which the Jobava London would be a shell of its current self. Let's illustrate this idea by considering one of Black's most natural developing moves.} O-O $2 {What could be more natural than castling? It transpires that this extremely natural move - as well as countless other setups that Black can adopt after Ne5 - is punished by a furious kingside offensive.} 2. g4 $1 {You will be playing this move countless times in your Jobava London career. White sends the g- and h-pawns up the board, something you are probably not used to doing in most of your current openings! The e5-knight secures the g4-pawn, making this move possible. In response to this move, Black typically has two options: 1) Taking the knight on e5, and following up by snapping off the g4-pawn (since we almost always recapture on e5 rather than on f5, which enables Black to dissolve our pawn structure with ...Bxf4), 2) Retreating the bishop to g6, which is the choice of most inexperienced players across most variations.} Bg6 3. h4 {At this point, you should begin to grasp the zen of White's idea. We threaten the prosaic h5, trapping the bishop (since ...Be4 is met with f2-f3). Black has two ways to parry the threat of h5: to create an escape square for the bishop with ...h6, and to meet us at the top with ...h5. Both of these moves contain two cardinal flaws: 1) Most importantly, the bishop on g6 loses the support of the h-pawn. This allows us to favorably trade our knight for Black's bishop, leading to the complete and utter ruination of Black's pawn structure, and creating a massive weakness on g6 that we can easily attack by deploying our bishop (or, in some cases, our queen) to f3. In situations where Black has not castled yet, this is almost always our response to ...h6 and ...h5, since there is no way to defend the g6-pawn other than to bring the king up to f7!  2) In situations where Black has already castled kingside, there is a sensible (and oftentimes more powerful) alternative to Nxg6: we can simply continue our kingside pawn storm with g4-g5, smashing open the h-file and, in most cases, generating an unstoppable attack against Black's king. In this particular position, option 2 is indeed the one that we choose after both ...h6 and ...h5 (see 3...Bf5 file).} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #2: Recapturing on d3 with the Pawn"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D02"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/pppn1ppp/4pn2/3p1b2/3P1B2/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "3"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{Our second idea involves an important positional idea that recurs in virtually all variations in which Black chooses to develop their light-squared bishop to f5 (this position, like the example from idea #1, comes from the 3...Bf5 variation). The first step is to deploy our light-squared bishop to d3, offering Black the option of swapping the bishops.} 1. Bd3 {Note that applying idea #1 here is ineffective, since Black already controls the e5-square with his own knight (we would, however, play Ne5 in response to ...Bd6, since Black would then not be able to recapture on e5 with the knight due to dxe5, forking the bishop on d6 and knight on f6).} Bxd3 {This is Black's most popular response to Bd3 in the majority of variations. Of course, there are alternatives: Black can choose to not respond or to drop the bishop back to g6. In 99% of cases in which Black chooses to take on d3, we recapture in the same way: with the pawn!} 2. cxd3 {This pawn structure, characterized by the central pawn mass, semi open c-file, and a pathway for the White to queen to b3, is one that you will come across countless times, and merits very careful study. Of course, blind adherence to chess principles dictate that this move is anti-positional, since it creates a pair of doubled pawns in the center. However, the virtues of recapturing with the pawn greatly outweigh its drawbacks. Indeed, this pawn structure offers us a litany of dangerous positional ideas on the queenside and in the center: 1) The most obvious set of follow-up ideas involve our newfound ability to find gainful employment for our heavy pieces. The a1-rook will find a home on c1, where it creates serious pressure on the c7-pawn. The queen, of course, will chill on b3, hitting the b7-pawn. Note that any movement of the Black pawns (particularly ...b6) creates serious and enduring queenside weaknesses that we can effectively exploit due to our general level of piece activity. 2) Bringing the queen to b3 is not the only idea we have at our disposal. In situations where Black moves the c-pawn up to c6, we can carry out a plan that you might find familiar from the Carlsbad variation/structure of the Queen's Gambit declined: we can set up a minority attack with a2-a3 (and/or Rb1) followed by b2-b4-b5, shattering Black's pawn structure and generating exciting prospects for our pieces.  3) The pawn on d3, far from being dead weight, is integral to the general health of our position. By securing the e4-square, it restricts the prospects of Black's f6-knight.  4) The d3-pawn also enables us to carry out a nasty plan of central expansion: we can continue with e3-e4, grabbing space and putting pressure on the d5-pawn. We don't have to stop there: in many cases, the pawn advances to e5, pushing Black's knight to the eighth rank and heavily clamping down on Black's position. As a result, Black will have an even harder time finding active prospects for his pieces. 5) Finally, the doubled pawns on the d-file frequently take the sting out of the typical pawn advance ...c7-c5, to which we can respond by capturing on c5 and regenerating our control of the center by pushing the other pawn up to d4 (the pawn can also remain on d3).  You will come across this recapture countless times across the various lines. Get used to it!} *

