[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #3: Kingside Expansion with h2-h4"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/n3pn2/1N1p4/3P1B2/4PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "1"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{The Jobava London is characterized by frequent play on all three sides of the board. Typical idea #3 involves a typical plan of kingside expansion involving g2-g4. But that is not the only type of kingside expansion that we come across. In situations where Black's light-squared bishop remains on c8, we can also initiate a kingside attack more directly with a move that outwardly appears brazen, but is in fact entirely justified by positional and tactical principles. This position, which stems from the 3...e6 variation, is a particularly instructive application of this idea:} 1. h4 $1 {This move truly epitomizes the spirit of the Jobava London! Your intuition may seem to indicate that there is no way a move like this can be truly justified, since we are essentially precluding our own ability to castle kingside, and we haven't even fully completed our development! Neither of these things correspond with reality. Of course, these types of positions are very concrete and should be studied primarily by consulting the analysis, but let's bring to the table a few important points that will help you get more comfortable with pushing the h-pawn: 1) White's attack comes very fast. It takes only two moves for the pawn to appear on h6, inducing massive and permanent weaknesses along the dark-squares. If Black halts the advance with ...h6, then the g-pawn can come to the rescue, rushing up to g5 and shattering Black's kingside. Note that our delaying of kingside castling is actually a good thing here, since the rook can still move to g1 and support the kingside pawn advance. 2) White's king is quite safe in the center, since Black has no easy way of carrying out any meaningful pawn breaks (again, this has to do with our firm control of the e5-square!). But there are plenty of situations when White can, and does, castle kingside. Just because the pawn is on h4 or h5 DOES NOT mean that it is no longer possible to castle kingside; yes, the pawn becomes somewhat of a weakness when it is no longer supported by the rook, but it cannot be easily attacked and the pawn shell remains generally intact. 3) In the 3...g6 variation, this move is particularly integral to White's strategy, since the advance of the h-pawn will cause an explosion once it reaches h5. Furthermore, in that variation you will find that we are more than happy to sacrifice an exchange on h5 (i.e. h5 Nxh5 Rxh5 gxh5 Qxh5), creating tremendous attacking prospects on the kingside.  4) This isn't a truly valid reason, but don't you feel like a badass making a move like this as early as move 5?! We certainly do. In all seriousness, unconventional play of this sort produces a strong psychological effect on the opponent, and can lead to an almost immediate loss of control. Behold the early advance of Harry the h-pawn!} *

