[Black "Idea #4: Queenside Blockade on the c5-square"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2rq1rk1/pp2ppb1/2n2np1/3pNb1p/B2P1B1P/P1N2P2/1PPQ2P1/R3K2R w KQ - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "3"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{[%evp 0,3,2,2,11,13]} {This position, which stems from the 3...g6 variation, furnishes a great example of an idea that recurs across several variations (particularly 3...e6 and 3...g6), and epitomizes White's ability to simultaneously assert control of the queenside and the kingside. It involves the following two steps: 1) First, we generate a hole on c5 by capturing either on a6 (in situations where Black has developed the knight to a6, generally in the 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 d5 3.Bf4 e6 4.Nb5 a6 - Black will later play ...c6 and the knight drops back to c3, creating the possibility of Bxa6) or, as in this case, on c6. Note that we are almost always happy to give up our light-squared bishop to make this happen. 2) Second, we attain control of the c5-square by hopping over to a4. We can then move on to c5, but that is not always necessary: it is control over the c5-square that we ultimately seek.} 1. Bxc6 $1 bxc6 2. Na4 {Why is it is so important to attain control over this square? The main reason is that it makes it virtually impossible for Black to achieve any degree of queenside activity. Without being able to carry out the ...c5 pawn break, Black cannot generate activity and has absolutely nothing to attack. But we are not simply trying to halt Black's queenside play: we can use the queenside for our own benefit, using the knight to put pressure on Black's weak pawns on the queenside. This is especially common in the 3...e6/...Na6 variation, where Black will be saddled with doubled a-pawns. The Jobava London is an opening in which we constantly intersperse tactical and positional ideas.  Get used to smashing open the kingside and applying positional ideas on the other side of the board, all at the same time!	} *

