[Black "Idea #5: Queen Transfer to h3"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppn2ppp/4pn2/2bpN3/5B2/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "17"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{This idea cannot truly be called "typical" since it really only occurs in one sub-variation of the 3...e6 line (in which Black responds to Nb5 with ...Na6 and then hides his knight on c7), but we have decided to include it because it truly captures the essence of this opening and demonstrates the jaw-dropping speed with which White can uncork a decisive on the kingside. In this position, we have already carried out the typical developmental scheme, putting the knight on e5 and training the bishop on the h7-pawn. There is one missing ingredient: we need the services of the lady.} 1. Qf3 $1 {The queen is heading for h3, where it will exert massive pressure on the h7-pawn. Most importantly, we delay the kingside pawn storm until Black moves the h- or g-pawns, which will create a hook and increase the effectiveness of a pawn storm. Let's consider what will happen if Black continues to develop without a care:} Bd7 2. Qh3 {Black's position is already virtually hopeless. We are threatening the straightforward Bg5-xf6, so Black has no choice but to weaken his kingside to prolong the king's life.} h6 {...g6 is typically a more resilient defensive method - see the 3...e6 file for more detailed analysis. This line, however, enables us to more clearly illustrate the potency of the queen:} 3. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 4. Qxh6 {Black has absolutely no way to kick the queen away, and we simultaneously threaten g4-g5, as well as the straightforward Nxd7, e.g.} Re8 {[%cal Rc5f8]} 5. Nxd7 $1 {Black has a rather unenviable choice between recapturing with the queen, allowing us to win back the knight, and taking with the knight:} Nxd7 {This allows us to demonstrate a classic and gorgeous mating pattern:} (5... Qxd7 6. Qxf6 $18) 6. Bh7+ Kh8 7. Bg6+ Kg8 8. Qh7+ Kf8 9. Qxf7# *

