[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #6: e3-e4 Pawn Break"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/5ppp/p3pn2/1ppp4/3P1B2/P1N1PN2/1PP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "5"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{Throughout the course, you will notice that White operates effectively on all three sides of the board: queenside, center, and kingside. Typical idea #1 involves using the center efficiently by maneuvering the knight to e5. But another very common idea involves engineering the central breakthrough e3-e4. Of course, we see the move e4 played in response to ...c5, but in most other lines - such as the 3...a6 variation - we prepare this break gradually, by deploying our bishop to d3 and queen to e2. First, we must end the central tension to prevent Black from pushing away our bishop with ...c4.} 1. dxc5 Bxc5 2. Bd3 {We are ready to push e3-e4, but Black does not have to cooperate.} Bb7 3. Qe2 {[%cal Re3e4] Very important. All ingredients are now in place for e4, but we do not have to rush - generally, it is a good idea to castle first so that our king is not caught in the crossfire once the center opens. Following that, we will be ready to push e4, opening the center and enabling us to more effectively take advantage of our superior piece activity. Once the d-file opens, the Black queen can also be pushed around with Rad1.} *

