[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #7: Knight Jump to b5"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp2ppbp/5np1/2pp4/3P1B1P/2N1P3/PPP2PP1/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "3"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{On move two of the Jobava London, we develop the knight to c3, violating the most basic principle of Queen's Pawn openings. Typical #1 showed one way in which we can compensate for that: by quickly activating our other knight. However, the queen's knight is far from a lost cause: on c3, it controls the crucial e4-square. But one of the most ubiquitous ideas in the Jobava London involves hopping to b5, threatening Nc7 and forcing Black to seriously compromise the placement of his own queen's knight. In this position, which obviously stems from the 3...g6 variation, Black has just carried out the standard ...c7-c5 pawn break. This affords us a perfect opportunity to initiate the knight jump:} 1. Nb5 {It may seem rather strange that a move which creates an ultra-prosaic threat is effective, but you should remember that we are not banking on Black missing Nc7 (although, in certain cases, such as the 3...Nc6 line, Black just actually does run into a situation where there is no defense against Nc7!). Rather, the point of this move is entirely positional.} Na6 2. c3 {We frequently follow up by solidifying the d4-pawn, London-style. In this position, the power of the knight jump is on full display: Black has no easy way to kick the knight out, and it can be further supported with a3-a4. In lines where Black's pawn is on c7 (particularly 3...e6), Black can force our knight out with ...c6, but this does not make Nb5 any less efficient, since Black will still have a hard time finding suitable prospects for his knight. The knight jump to b5 is yet another illustration of the multifaceted nature of the Jobava London, which features countless ideas that are both positional and tactical.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #8: Dealing with ...Bb4 and ...Ne4"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/p1p1pn2/3p4/1b1P1B2/2N1P3/PPP2PPP/R2QK1NR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "7"]
[EventDate "2023.??.??"]

{Black has a wide choice when it comes to the deployment of his dark-squared bishop. Most of the time, it is developed to either e7 or d6, but in some variations (such as in the line 3...e6 4.Nb5 Na6 5.e3 c6 6.Nc3, which is the one in front of you), Black can also develop the bishop more aggressively to b4. The instinctual reaction here is a2-a3, but it turns out that the so-called "threat" - to pressure our knight with ...Ne4 - requires absolutely no action on our part. As long as we can castle in response to ...Ne4 (to avoid losing an exchange), we are more than happy to part ways with a pawn in return for a tremendous initiative and total control over the queenside.} 1. Nf3 Ne4 2. O-O $1 {The key.} Nxc3 {Black can also take with the bishop first, but that changes very little (see analysis).} 3. bxc3 Bxc3 4. Rb1 {It is clear, even to an untrained eye, that White's compensation is nothing short of massive. The bishop on c3 is vulnerable, the b-file is ours, the other bishop has no prospects, and we can pile on the pressure in several obvious ways, such as Qd3 and Ne5. Some of these things are unique to this position, but in almost all variations, Black faces tremendous difficulties upon capturing the pawn on e3. Don't be afraid to sacrifice this pawn!} *

